# Vibe lane

Installed only by `/setup-vibe` into this clone. Not part of any developer preset. Do not commit this file. Do not stage `.cursor/rules/business-team-experiments.mdc`.

Architecture, craft, and testing rules in this project's `.cursor/rules/` still apply. This file decides the lane.

## Plain prompt

When the user did not invoke `/feature-start`:

1. Before any edit, `git fetch` the latest `main`.
2. If the current branch is `feature/business-ideas/<topic>`, stay on it. Integrate `main` into this branch. Do not check out `main` to do the work.
3. Otherwise create and checkout `feature/business-ideas/<short-kebab>` from `main`. Reuse an existing topic name when the user is continuing that idea.
4. Untracked local files are allowed. Stop only on a real merge, rebase, or checkout conflict.
5. Do not follow `feature-workflow`. No requirements confirmation, no design-doc gate, no multi-critic fix loop, no three-cycle stop.
6. Write or update the tests the stack rules call for. Run them. If they fail, record the command and the failure, then keep going.
7. Do not commit, push, or open a pull request. That is `/submit`.
8. Do not push or merge `main`.

Reply with what changed, where it lives, which tests ran, and any failures. Point them at `/submit` when they want a developer to review, and at `/feature-start` only if they want the full developer ceremony first.

## /feature-start

Follow `feature-workflow` for this session, including its pauses and the critic fix loop. The branch may be `feature/…` or `fix/…`.

Still do not push or merge `main`. At the handoff, tell the user to run `/submit`. Do not tell them to run `/commit-push`.

## /submit

`/submit` is the only commit, push, and pull-request step. It may run on `feature/business-ideas/…`, `feature/…`, or `fix/…`. It must refuse `main`.
